# ========================================================= 4. DISCUSSION =
H("4. Discussion", level=1)

P(
    "In this study, we extended the DyAM multimodal attention "
    "framework with two complementary contributions: a competitive "
    "(OvO) attention mechanism with fewer parameters and an "
    "NLP-based encoding of tabular clinical features, and evaluated "
    "both using binary classification and survival endpoints in a "
    "247-patient cohort."
)
P(
    "The strongest configurations, cooperative and OvO attention "
    "with NLP-encoded clinical variables at the primary benchmark "
    "(Rad+IHC-G+Gen+PDL1), reached mean AUCs of 0.786 ± 0.017 and "
    "0.785 ± 0.022 over 5 seeds × 10-fold, and ensemble AUCs of "
    "0.799 and 0.792. These figures place the models close to the AUC "
    "of 0.80 reported for the original framework [15], and above "
    "PD-L1 TPS alone (0.720) and TMB alone (0.616) in the same "
    "cohort, consistent with the modest discrimination reported for "
    "these markers [7,8]; the interval, however, is wide (± 0.062) "
    "and includes values below 0.75. A harder evaluation gave lower "
    "estimates: with training on the patients with the earliest CT "
    "accession numbers and testing on the later ones (a proxy for "
    "time, as calendar dates were not available), OvO+NLP reached "
    "an AUC of 0.703 (95% bootstrap interval 0.587–0.819) and "
    "DyAM+NLP 0.700 (0.587–0.812) on the 124 later patients (21 "
    "responders); at seven sources the values were 0.707 and 0.678. "
    "The direction matches the cross-validated comparison at seven "
    "sources but not at the primary benchmark, and the intervals "
    "overlap almost completely. The cross-validated figure should "
    "therefore not be read as the expected performance in new "
    "patients. Further, the 247 patients were used to compare "
    "roughly 60 architecture and training variants during "
    "development, so the largest figures partly reflect selection; "
    "we estimate this optimism at 0.014–0.031 AUC (expected maximum of 52 candidates with no true difference, assuming a correlation of 0.8 to 0 between candidates, from the standard error of ensemble AUC differences between closely related models)."
)
P(
    "**OvO versus cooperative attention.** Under the "
    "repeated-seed protocol OvO had the higher mean AUC at the large "
    "configurations (BM1: 0.771 against 0.763; Rad+IHC-A+MutAmp, "
    "Rad+IHC-A+Gen+PDL1 and Rad+IHC-A+Gen+TMB+PDL1 by +0.019 to "
    "+0.021, each in all five seeds) and at TMB+PDL1 (+0.045), while "
    "the cooperative model was higher at IHC-G+Gen (−0.028) and by "
    "smaller margins in a few radiomics-containing configurations; "
    "the two formulations coincide in the single-source "
    "configurations. With the NLP encoding in place of the numeric "
    "labs, the two mechanisms reached 0.786 and 0.785 at BM2, and "
    "OvO+NLP was higher than DyAM+NLP in all five seeds at four of "
    "the six configurations with five or more sources to which the "
    "NLP source was added (Rad+IHC-A+Gen, Rad+IHC-A+MutAmp, "
    "Rad+IHC-A+Gen+PDL1 and Rad+IHC-A+Gen+TMB+PDL1; +0.010 to "
    "+0.028), with the cooperative model nominally higher at the "
    "other two (Rad+IHC-G+Gen, −0.004; BM1, −0.001). The "
    "patient-level bootstrap intervals of the ensemble differences "
    "were wide and included zero in four of the six configurations. "
    "All of these differences are of the same order as the "
    "variability between seeds (≈ 0.02 AUC); we therefore describe "
    "the result as a consistent tendency in this cohort and not as "
    "a demonstrated superiority. A plausible explanation is parameter economy: "
"superiority. A plausible explanation is parameter economy: "
    "with the 384-dimensional NLP source the cooperative model had "
    "4,969 trainable parameters against 1,242 for OvO, and the "
    "advantage of OvO was largest at the highest source counts. A "
    "learning-curve analysis supports this indirectly: as the "
    "training set grew from about 89 to 222 patients, the AUC of "
    "OvO+NLP rose from 0.707 to 0.785 and that of DyAM+NLP from "
    "0.677 to 0.786, so the gap narrowed from 0.030 to −0.002 "
    "(DyAM+NLP marginally higher at the full size) and neither "
    "curve had levelled off, as expected if sample size limits what "
    "can be learned and the smaller model helps most where data are "
    "scarcest. We did not test the parameter-economy explanation "
    "directly. In a leave-one-source-out analysis of OvO+NLP at the "
    "primary benchmark (Supplementary Note S4), removing any single "
    "source lowered the mean AUC in 4 or 5 of 5 seeds, by "
    "0.013 (NLP-encoded clinical) to 0.029 (pathology), "
    "and an availability vector (which sources a "
    "patient has) raised the AUC by 0.015–0.017 in the two Gen "
    "configurations without PD-L1 but by only −0.003 to +0.008 in "
    "the others (Supplementary Note S5); it was not part of the "
    "main model."
)
P(
    "**NLP encoding of clinical variables.** The finding that held "
    "in both analysis pipelines was that the NLP encoding exceeded "
    "numeric labs: by +0.017 and +0.020 in the two pathology arms "
    "under cooperative attention and by +0.014 to +0.024 in the two "
    "seven-source configurations containing labs under both "
    "attention mechanisms. We propose that sentence embeddings "
    "capture non-linear interactions between clinical variables –- "
    "for example, the combined implication of advanced age, low "
    "albumin, and poor performance status –- that are not "
    "explicitly represented in a 13-dimensional numeric vector, "
    "whereas the attention mechanism would otherwise have to learn "
    "these interactions from a small training set [16,17]. This is "
    "a hypothesis; our data do not test it. By contrast, the gain "
    "over omitting clinical variables altogether was smaller and "
    "varied between analyses (+0.007 in the arm analysis; +0.014 to "
    "+0.023 at the primary benchmark of the 21-configuration "
    "analysis), of the same order as the partition-to-partition "
    "spread; we therefore do not claim a benefit of NLP encoding "
    "over leaving out clinical variables. The domain-specific "
    "BioClinBERT encoder was lower than the general-purpose "
    "sentence encoder in all five partitions (Supplementary Note "
    "S2)."
)
P(
    "**Survival analysis.** The NLP encodings raised the hazard "
    "ratio and the Kaplan–Meier χ² of the risk score relative to no "
    "clinical variables (for example 4.12 → 4.43–4.52 in the IHC-A "
    "arm, where numeric labs gave 4.13), but the NLP encodings were "
    "within the partition-to-partition spread of numeric labs on "
    "these endpoints, and the C-index was 0.61–0.62 for every "
    "variant. Time-dependent AUC at 18 months was highest "
    "without clinical encoding, and we do not retain the "
    "interpretation that NLP encoding captures prognostic rather "
    "than predictive information. Time-dependent AUC at 6, 12 and "
    "18 months was likewise within about 0.03 across encodings. At "
    "the primary benchmark, OvO attention with the NLP encoding gave "
    "a higher hazard ratio than cooperative attention in every seed "
    "(log hazard ratio +0.070) with an almost unchanged C-index "
    "(+0.006); each factor alone contributed about half of this "
    "gain. We did not test why, and one plausible reason is the "
    "smaller parameter count of OvO when a 384-dimensional source "
    "is added."
)
P(
    "From a clinical implementation perspective, NLP encoding of "
    "structured clinical data offers practical advantages: encoding "
    "a patient profile requires less than one second on CPU, "
    "requires no labelled clinical text, and can be applied to any "
    "structured electronic health record (EHR) field that can be "
    "rendered as a sentence. This makes the approach transferable "
    "to other structured clinical datasets without requiring a "
    "fine-tuned clinical language model."
)
P(
    "**Limitations.** First, this was a single-institution "
    "retrospective cohort, which may limit generalisability and "
    "introduce selection bias. Second, with n = 247 (62 responders) "
    "the 95% interval for an AUC is about ± 0.07, and detecting a "
    "true difference of 0.02 AUC with 80% power would require "
    "approximately 1,200–1,350 patients (Supplementary Note S3); "
    "the differences between models reported here are smaller than "
    "this cohort can resolve, and they are described by direction "
    "and magnitude for that reason. Third, results depended on the "
    "numerical environment (in a reproduction check of the original "
    "publication's primary benchmark the same code and library versions "
    "gave 0.798 on Linux and 0.788 on Windows, so all results are "
    "reported from one pinned "
    "environment), and the repeated-seed analyses use the same "
    "patients in every partition, so they quantify partition "
    "variability, not variability across patients. Fourth, the "
    "sentence encoder was applied in a zero-shot manner without "
    "fine-tuning on NSCLC-specific clinical text. Fifth, no "
    "calendar dates were available, so temporal validation could "
    "only be approximated by CT accession order, and the two models "
    "were compared in only two configurations. Finally, PD-L1 TPS "
    "was scored using the Sauter method in this cohort, which may "
    "differ from the SP142 or 22C3 assays used at other "
    "institutions, and the generalisability of PD-L1-derived "
    "features should be interpreted with this in mind."
)

# ========================================================= 5. CONCLUSIONS =
H("5. Conclusions", level=1)
P(
    "We extended the DyAM multimodal attention framework for ICI "
    "response prediction in NSCLC with a competitive (OvO) "
    "attention mechanism and an NLP-based encoding of structured "
    "clinical variables. Under repeated 10-fold partitions (5 seeds "
    "× 10-fold), OvO attention had a higher mean AUC than "
    "cooperative attention at the primary benchmark (0.771 against "
    "0.763) and in most configurations fusing five or more sources, "
    "with about a quarter of the trainable parameters; with the NLP "
    "encoding, OvO and cooperative attention reached 0.785 ± 0.022 "
    "and 0.786 ± 0.017 at the primary benchmark. These differences "
    "are of the same order as the variability between seeds and the "
    "patient-level intervals are wide, so they indicate a consistent "
    "tendency in "
    "this cohort rather than an established advantage. NLP encoding "
    "of clinical variables with a general-purpose sentence "
    "transformer exceeded numeric labs by +0.017 to +0.020 AUC in "
    "both pathology arms and by +0.014 to +0.024 in both "
    "seven-source configurations containing labs, and raised the "
    "Cox hazard ratio of the risk score relative to omitting "
    "clinical variables, but not reliably beyond numeric labs; its "
    "advantage over omitting clinical variables altogether was "
    "small and is unresolved. An order-based hold-out evaluation "
    "gave lower estimates (0.703 for OvO+NLP and 0.700 for "
    "DyAM+NLP), so cross-validated AUCs should not be read as "
    "expected performance in new patients. Validation in larger, "
    "multi-institutional cohorts with calendar dates is needed to "
    "confirm these findings and to establish their clinical utility "
    "for treatment selection."
)

